"""Chapter 1 — Introduction (~10,000 words) — v2 with customer service component and correct LLM stack"""

def chapter_one():
    title = "CHAPTER 1. INTRODUCTION"
    def S(t): return {'type': 'body', 'text': t}
    def H2(t): return {'type': 'h2', 'text': t}
    def H3(t): return {'type': 'h3', 'text': t}
    sections = [
        H2("1.1 Background and Motivation"),
        S("Hospitals in sub-Saharan Africa bear a dual administrative burden that most high-income country healthcare systems have largely automated. Patients must navigate complex multi-step processes simply to book an appointment, and once they arrive at the facility, they encounter a second challenge: obtaining basic but essential information about the hospital's services, departments, fees, contacts, and operating procedures. Both challenges are costly in time, dignity, and healthcare outcomes. A patient who cannot determine which department handles a specific condition, or who does not know the hospital's visiting hours or accepted payment methods, is a patient whose care pathway is interrupted before clinical care even begins. A patient who cannot book an appointment from home, from work, or outside office hours is a patient whose access to timely care depends on their ability to physically present themselves at a registration desk at the right moment. These are not peripheral service quality issues; they are core access barriers that shape who receives healthcare and when."),
        S("Kenyatta University Teaching, Referral and Research Hospital (KUTRRH) in Nairobi, Kenya is one of the largest national referral hospitals in East Africa, serving a patient population of several hundred thousand visits annually across more than thirty clinical departments. Its administrative front end, which manages appointment booking, patient information queries, registration support, and general customer service, handles an enormous volume of interactions that are currently served almost entirely by in-person queuing and telephone contact. The telephone-based channel is constrained by office hours, by the number of available staff to answer calls, and by the language competencies of available staff. The in-person channel is constrained by geography, working hours, and the transaction cost imposed on patients who must travel to the facility to perform an administrative task. Both channels operate primarily in English, creating a further access barrier for patients whose most fluent language is Swahili or a regional language."),
        S("Conversational artificial intelligence offers a technically credible approach to reducing these access barriers. A multilingual conversational AI system that is available around the clock, accessible from any mobile device, capable of booking appointments, answering hospital information queries, and providing queue congestion guidance in both English and Swahili would address the primary dimensions of the access problem. The technical feasibility of building such a system has increased rapidly since the introduction of large language models capable of broad conversational competence across task types and languages. However, feasibility does not imply readiness for healthcare deployment. Healthcare administrative AI faces specific challenges in reliability, language consistency, information accuracy, and governance that general-purpose conversational AI systems do not automatically satisfy. This thesis addresses those challenges directly through a design science research approach that produces a working artifact, evaluates it rigorously, and documents the design knowledge needed to build reliable, equitable, and governance-ready healthcare conversational AI systems."),
        H2("1.2 Problem Statement"),
        S("The problem this thesis addresses has two interlocking dimensions that compound each other in practice. The first dimension concerns appointment scheduling. Manual appointment booking processes in Kenyan public hospitals require patients to contact the hospital by telephone or in person during office hours, navigate a registration system that may require multiple attempts due to line congestion or staff unavailability, and receive appointment confirmation through informal channels such as a verbal confirmation or a handwritten note. This process systematically disadvantages patients with inflexible work schedules, patients in peri-urban or rural locations, patients with hearing impairments, and patients whose English language proficiency is limited. The patient who most needs timely care is frequently the patient least able to navigate this administrative process successfully."),
        S("The second dimension concerns hospital information access and customer support. Patients regularly need information about the hospital before, during, and after clinical episodes: which departments treat which conditions, what the accepted payment methods are, whether specific insurance schemes are accepted, what the visiting hours policy is, who leads specific departments, what the hospital contact numbers are, and how to escalate a service complaint. This information is not consistently available through accessible channels. The hospital website, where available, may be incomplete, outdated, or navigable only to users with strong English reading proficiency and reliable internet access. Telephone inquiry requires availability during office hours and imposes wait times. In-person inquiry requires physical presence. The patient who needs hospital information most urgently may be least positioned to access it through existing channels."),
        S("Critically, the appointment management and customer support problems are not independent. A patient initiating an appointment booking often needs customer support information first: which department to book with, whether the service is covered by their insurance, what preparation is required. A patient using the customer support function may naturally progress to booking an appointment once their informational questions are resolved. Building these two functions into a single coherent conversational interface is therefore not merely a convenience but an architectural reflection of how patients actually navigate healthcare administrative processes. The design challenge is to integrate both functions reliably and equitably, in multiple languages, within a single system that maintains accurate transaction outcomes and factually grounded information responses."),
        H2("1.3 Research Objectives"),
        S("The overarching aim of this research is to demonstrate that AI-driven conversational systems can serve as reliable, equitable, and governance-ready administrative support tools for patients in Kenyan public hospital contexts, addressing both appointment management and hospital customer support within a single multilingual interface. This overarching aim is operationalized through five specific research objectives, each of which targets a distinct dimension of the design and evaluation problem. The objectives are cumulative: each builds on the platform established by the preceding objective, and the system that results from their combined achievement represents a contribution that none of the individual objectives could produce in isolation."),
        H3("Objective 1: Design and Implement a Dual-Function Conversational AI Architecture"),
        S("The first objective is to design and implement a hybrid conversational AI architecture for Kenyan hospital contexts that integrates an appointment management subsystem and a retrieval-augmented generation customer support subsystem within a single coherent conversational interface. Achieving this objective requires making and documenting a series of architectural decisions about how two functionally distinct subsystems, one transactional and one informational, can share a conversational layer, a multilingual engine, and a governance framework without architectural conflict. The appointment management subsystem must execute structured booking transactions reliably, which calls for deterministic workflow control. The customer support subsystem must deliver factually accurate hospital information, which calls for document-grounded retrieval rather than unguided language model generation. The challenge is to route patient queries correctly between these two subsystems based on query intent, maintaining coherent conversational context as patients move naturally between informational and transactional needs within a single session. The outcome expected from this objective is a working prototype implemented in Python using the LangChain and LangGraph frameworks, with intent routing, shared session state, and coherent language handling operating correctly across both functional modes. The architectural design knowledge produced by this objective is intended to be replicable by other developers building comparable dual-function healthcare AI systems."),
        H3("Objective 2: Implement and Evaluate Deterministic Booking Guardrails"),
        S("The second objective is to implement and evaluate deterministic guardrail mechanisms that ensure reliable completion of appointment transactions regardless of probabilistic variation in the language model component. The motivation for this objective is the well-documented tendency of large language models to generate tool invocations that are missing required parameters, contain structurally invalid parameter values, or fail to follow the sequential information collection logic required for a multi-step transactional workflow. In healthcare contexts, these failures translate directly into booking failures that deny patients the appointment they attempted to secure. The deterministic guardrail approach addresses this by interposing a validation layer between the language model's suggested tool invocations and their actual execution: no booking transaction is executed unless all required parameters have been collected, validated for structural correctness, and confirmed by the patient. This objective requires designing the validation logic, implementing it within a LangGraph state machine that enforces the correct information collection sequence, and evaluating the guardrail's effectiveness by comparing booking completion rates before and after its introduction. The expected outcome is a demonstrated improvement in booking completion reliability from a pre-guardrail baseline, with the post-guardrail system achieving near-complete elimination of parameter-driven booking failures in controlled testing."),
        H3("Objective 3: Implement and Evaluate a RAG-Based Customer Support Subsystem"),
        S("The third objective is to implement and evaluate a knowledge-base customer support subsystem that enables accurate, document-grounded responses to patient information queries about hospital services, departments, fees, contacts, visiting hours, and operational procedures. The fundamental challenge this objective addresses is the inadequacy of unaided large language model responses for institution-specific information queries. A language model trained on general internet text may have no reliable knowledge of KUTRRH's current fee schedules, specific departmental contacts, visiting hours policies, or management structure, and is liable to generate plausible-sounding but incorrect answers when asked about these matters. The retrieval-augmented generation approach resolves this by building a searchable index of official KUTRRH documents, retrieving the passages most relevant to each patient query using semantic embedding similarity, and instructing the language model to synthesize its answer only from retrieved content with explicit acknowledgment of uncertainty when relevant information is not present in the retrieved passages. This objective requires building the FAISS vector index with HuggingFace sentence transformer embeddings, constructing the synthesis prompt that enforces source grounding, and evaluating the subsystem for factual accuracy, source attribution, and appropriate handling of out-of-coverage queries. The expected outcome is a customer support subsystem whose responses are verifiably grounded in official hospital documents, achieving a level of factual reliability that unaided language model generation cannot match for institution-specific queries."),
        H3("Objective 4: Integrate Queue Congestion Prediction into the Booking Workflow"),
        S("The fourth objective is to integrate queue congestion prediction into the appointment booking workflow to provide patients with decision-relevant information about expected waiting times at different appointment slots. This objective addresses a gap in the value that appointment booking tools typically provide: most systems allow patients to book an available slot but do not help patients choose between available slots in a way that minimizes their expected wait. Queuing theory, as reviewed in Chapter 2, provides analytical models that predict queue congestion as a function of service type, time of day, and day of week. These models can be operationalized within the booking interface to score candidate appointment slots by predicted congestion level and present the results as plain-language recommendations that guide patients toward less congested times without requiring any understanding of queuing mathematics. Achieving this objective requires implementing the prediction scoring function with empirically grounded parameter values for each service type, designing the recommendation presentation format to be interpretable and honest about prediction uncertainty, and evaluating whether patients presented with recommendations in the scripted test environment select lower-congestion slots at a meaningful rate. The expected outcome is a recommendation feature that activates reliably, presents congestion information in a format accessible to patients regardless of technical background, and demonstrably influences slot selection behavior in a direction consistent with reduced peak demand."),
        H3("Objective 5: Evaluate the System and Document Transferable Design Knowledge"),
        S("The fifth objective is to evaluate the complete system against a comprehensive test scenario battery across five analytical dimensions, and to document the design knowledge produced by the research in a form suitable for replication and adaptation by other developers and institutions. The evaluation objective is essential to converting the design work into scholarship: a working prototype that has not been rigorously evaluated provides anecdotal rather than evidential support for its design claims. The 47-scenario test battery operationalizes each of the five analytical dimensions, namely accessibility, reliability, optimization, trust, and governance, into specific testable scenarios that probe the system's behavior under both standard and edge-case conditions. Evaluation results are expressed as quantitative metrics, including booking completion rates, intent classification accuracy, customer support factual accuracy, multilingual consistency rates, and governance compliance rates, as well as qualitative assessments of interaction quality and uncertainty handling. The documentation objective ensures that the knowledge produced by the research is communicated at a level of specificity enabling other developers to replicate the architectural patterns, evaluation framework, and governance approach in different healthcare contexts, languages, and institutional settings. Together, the evaluation and documentation components transform the prototype from a demonstration of feasibility into a contribution to the healthcare AI design knowledge base with practical value for developers, institutions, and policy-makers beyond the immediate research context."),
        S("The five objectives are structured to produce two distinct types of contribution: an artifact contribution (Objectives 1 through 4, which produce the working system) and a knowledge contribution (Objective 5, which produces the evaluation evidence and documented design patterns). Both types of contribution are necessary for design science research, which treats the design and evaluation of artifacts as a form of knowledge production rather than as mere technical deliverables. The objectives collectively ensure that the thesis produces not only a system that works but also evidence that it works, an explanation of why it works, and documentation sufficient for others to replicate or adapt the approach."),
        H2("1.4 Research Questions"),
        S("Five research questions guide the investigation. Each question is grounded in a specific dimension of the design and evaluation problem identified in the problem statement, and each is answered through a combination of design decisions, implementation choices, and evaluation evidence presented in subsequent chapters. The questions are stated here at the level of generality appropriate for an introductory chapter; Chapter 3 operationalizes each question into specific evaluation criteria and scenario categories that make the questions empirically tractable. Together the five questions span the full scope of the research: system architecture, transactional reliability, informational accuracy, optimization, and governance."),
        H3("RQ1: Architecture for Dual-Function Healthcare Administrative AI"),
        S("The first research question asks: what hybrid architecture enables a conversational AI system to reliably handle both appointment transactions and knowledge-grounded customer support within a single interface designed for a Kenyan hospital context? This question is the foundational architectural question of the thesis. It does not ask whether AI can support appointment booking or hospital information delivery in isolation, since both capabilities have been demonstrated separately in the published literature, but rather how they can be combined within a single coherent system that is appropriate for the specific institutional, linguistic, and resource context of Kenyan public hospitals. The question requires the research to address how a transactional subsystem that needs deterministic control can coexist with an informational subsystem that needs retrieval-based response generation, how intent detection and routing between the two subsystems should be designed, how shared session state should be managed across functional mode transitions, and what the minimum technical infrastructure requirements are for deployment in a resource-constrained public hospital context. Answering this question is the primary purpose of Chapter 4 and the architectural evaluation component of Chapter 5."),
        H3("RQ2: Deterministic Guardrails for Booking Reliability"),
        S("The second research question asks: how can deterministic guardrail mechanisms be implemented within a large language model workflow to achieve reliable appointment booking completion in a healthcare setting? This question is motivated by the core technical limitation identified in the problem analysis: purely generative AI systems produce tool invocations with missing or invalid parameters at a non-trivial rate, and in healthcare appointment booking, each such failure represents a patient who did not receive the booking they attempted to make. The question asks not only whether guardrails can improve reliability, a claim that is theoretically straightforward, but how they should be designed and at what point in the workflow they should be applied to eliminate the systematic parameter-omission failure mode without disrupting conversational flow or imposing an unacceptable burden of clarifying questions on the patient. The question also encompasses the design of the state machine that enforces information collection sequencing and the validation logic that checks parameter completeness and structural correctness before any booking execution. Chapter 4 addresses the design of these mechanisms and Chapter 5 provides the empirical evidence comparing pre-guardrail and post-guardrail booking completion rates."),
        H3("RQ3: RAG for Accurate Hospital Information Delivery"),
        S("The third research question asks: how can retrieval-augmented generation be implemented to deliver accurate, source-grounded hospital information responses to patient customer support queries in a multilingual conversational interface? This question targets the informational accuracy challenge that unaided language model generation cannot reliably meet for institution-specific queries. It encompasses four specific design questions: how should hospital documents be processed, segmented, and indexed to support semantic retrieval at query time; what embedding model should be used to represent both document passages and patient queries in a shared semantic vector space that supports cross-lingual retrieval for both English and Swahili patient queries against predominantly English documents; how should the synthesis prompt be designed to constrain the language model to document-grounded responses while maintaining natural conversational quality; and how should the system behave when the knowledge base does not contain information sufficient to answer a query, to avoid hallucination while still providing useful guidance to the patient. Chapter 4 documents the specific design choices made for each of these questions, and Chapter 5 evaluates factual accuracy, source attribution, and uncertainty handling in customer support scenarios."),
        H3("RQ4: Queue-Aware Slot Recommendations"),
        S("The fourth research question asks: how can queue congestion prediction be integrated into a conversational appointment booking system to provide interpretable slot recommendations that influence patient appointment timing decisions? This question builds on the queuing theory literature reviewed in Chapter 2, which establishes that healthcare queue congestion is substantially predictable from service type, time of day, and day of week, and asks how these predictions can be operationalized as actionable guidance within a booking conversation. The question has two components: a design component that concerns how the prediction model should be structured, calibrated, and presented to produce recommendations that are both accurate in relative terms and comprehensible to patients without specialist knowledge of operations research; and an evaluation component that concerns how the effect of recommendations on slot selection behavior should be measured in a controlled evaluation context. The question also encompasses the language of recommendation presentation, including how uncertainty should be honestly communicated without undermining the recommendation's usefulness, and how recommendation uptake should be distinguished from coercion by preserving the patient's explicit right to select any available slot regardless of the system's suggestion. Chapter 4 describes the prediction model and presentation design; Chapter 5 evaluates recommendation quality and uptake."),
        H3("RQ5: Governance for Multilingual Healthcare AI in Kenya"),
        S("The fifth research question asks: what governance framework is appropriate for a multilingual conversational AI system that handles both transactional appointment management and informational customer support in a Kenyan public healthcare institution? This question is broader than the other four in that it concerns not only technical design choices but the institutional, regulatory, and ethical context within which those choices must be situated. It requires the research to identify the specific governance requirements that apply in Kenya, including the obligations imposed by the Data Protection Act (2019), and to translate those requirements into specific system design features including audit logging standards, data minimization requirements, scope enforcement mechanisms, escalation pathways, and language equity provisions. The question also concerns the governance of the dual-function system specifically: governing a system that handles both personal transactional data, the booking records that contain patient identity and contact information, and informational queries that do not require personal data collection, calls for differentiated data handling procedures that are not always addressed in general AI governance frameworks. Chapter 4 describes the governance controls implemented and Chapter 5 evaluates compliance against the full set of governance requirements identified."),
        S("The five research questions are interconnected rather than independent. Answering RQ1 about architecture depends on answers to RQ2, RQ3, and implicitly RQ5, since the architecture cannot be finalized without decisions about how reliability, information accuracy, and governance are addressed within it. Answering RQ2 through RQ4 is constrained by RQ5, since governance requirements may exclude design approaches that would otherwise be technically effective. RQ4 can only be answered within the context of a booking system that has already addressed RQ2, since an unreliable booking system cannot meaningfully add queue recommendations to an already-failing core transaction workflow. This interdependence is intentional: the five questions are designed to map jointly onto a single integrated artifact, and the research design ensures that each question is answered as part of the artifact's development and evaluation rather than as a separate study. The integration of answers across all five questions is the primary contribution of the synthesis presented in Chapters 6 and 7."),
        H2("1.5 Significance of the Research"),
        S("The significance of this research operates across four levels. At the patient access level, the research demonstrates a technically verified approach to reducing the administrative barriers to healthcare access for patients in Kenya, with specific attention to barriers related to language, time, and geography. The customer support component is particularly significant because hospital information is a fundamental input to patient health decision-making: patients who do not know which service to seek, where to go, what to bring, or what to pay cannot make good decisions about their care pathway regardless of the quality of clinical care available to them. By integrating accurate, document-grounded customer support with appointment booking in a single multilingual conversational interface, the system addresses the full pre-visit administrative pathway rather than only the booking transaction."),
        S("At the design knowledge level, the research contributes a replicable architecture for combining transactional AI (guardrailed LangGraph workflow) and informational AI (retrieval-augmented generation) within a single healthcare conversational interface implemented in Python. The integration of these two architectural patterns within a unified intent-routed conversational system, with shared multilingual capability and shared governance controls, is a novel contribution to the healthcare AI design literature. The design knowledge is documented at sufficient implementation detail to enable direct replication by developers with Python programming competence and access to the required open-source toolchain, which includes LangChain, LangGraph, FAISS, and HuggingFace sentence transformers."),
        S("At the language equity level, the research demonstrates that a well-designed conversational AI system can provide equivalent quality of service in both English and Swahili across both appointment management and customer support functions without requiring separate systems for each language. This demonstration is significant for the approximately 45 million speakers of Swahili across East Africa for whom English-dominant digital health services represent a systematic access barrier. The approach taken, combining LLM-based multilingual generation with deterministic template override for transaction-critical outputs, is a practical and replicable pattern for multilingual healthcare AI design."),
        S("At the governance and policy level, the research contributes a practical governance framework for healthcare AI deployment in Kenya that is specifically designed around the requirements of the Data Protection Act (2019) and the institutional realities of public hospitals. The governance framework addresses audit logging, data minimization, scope enforcement, escalation pathways, and language equity requirements for a system that handles both transactional patient data and knowledge-base information queries."),
        H2("1.6 The Research Context: KUTRRH"),
        S("Kenyatta University Teaching, Referral and Research Hospital was established in 2018 and serves as a Level 6 national referral hospital under Kenya's health system classification. The hospital operates more than thirty clinical departments including general medicine, cardiology, oncology, orthopedics, pediatrics, obstetrics and gynecology, ophthalmology, ear nose and throat, neurology, nephrology, urology, dentistry, dermatology, psychiatry, radiology, and laboratory services. The hospital also maintains a pharmacy, physiotherapy, and occupational therapy services, as well as specialized units for intensive care, dialysis, and bone marrow transplant. This breadth of services creates a substantial patient information challenge: the variety of services is large enough that many patients cannot easily determine without guidance which department handles their clinical need, what the referral pathway requirements are, or what preparation is needed before specific service appointments."),
        S("The hospital serves a socioeconomically and linguistically diverse patient population. The majority of patients are Kenyan nationals, and the patient population includes both Nairobi metropolitan residents and patients referred from other counties across Kenya. Language diversity is substantial: while official communications are in English, the majority of patients are most comfortable communicating in Swahili, and a significant fraction are more comfortable in regional languages. The hospital's administrative interactions are predominantly in English, creating a language barrier for the fraction of patients whose English proficiency is limited. The multilingual design requirements of the AI assistant are directly motivated by this patient population characteristic. The hospital's customer service function, encompassing responses to inquiries about services, fees, contacts, departments, and operational procedures, currently relies on staff who may not always be available or consistent in the information they provide, creating a further motivation for a knowledge-base-grounded automated customer support channel."),
        H2("1.7 Overview of the System"),
        S("The system designed and evaluated in this thesis is a multilingual AI hospital assistant implemented in Python using the LangChain and LangGraph frameworks, with Meta's Llama 3.3 70B large language model accessed through the Groq cloud inference API as the conversational intelligence component. The system is accessible through a Streamlit web interface that provides a familiar chat-based interaction modality suitable for both mobile and desktop access."),
        S("The appointment management function enables patients to book new appointments by providing their name, patient identification number, telephone number, and email address, and selecting a service type and preferred date. The system presents the top five optimized appointment slots based on machine-learning-generated queue congestion predictions, allows the patient to select a slot, and generates a deterministic booking confirmation in the patient's language of interaction. It also supports appointment cancellation, appointment viewing, and slot recommendation queries that show the least congested times for a given service and date."),
        S("The customer support function enables patients to ask natural-language questions about the hospital and receive accurate, document-grounded responses drawn from a vector-indexed knowledge base of official KUTRRH documents including the hospital's service charter, departmental guides, fee schedules, contact directories, and management information. The knowledge base is built using FAISS vector indexing with HuggingFace sentence transformer embeddings, and responses are synthesized by the language model from retrieved document passages with source attribution. This retrieval-augmented generation approach ensures that responses about hospital information are grounded in official documents rather than generated from the language model's general training knowledge, which may be incomplete or out of date regarding specific institutional details."),
        S("An intent detection and routing mechanism determines whether an incoming patient query is directed to the appointment management pathway or the customer support pathway. This routing ensures that the system's response strategy is appropriate for the query type, with transactional queries receiving the guardrailed deterministic booking workflow and informational queries receiving the retrieval-augmented generation knowledge base response pathway. A patient can move between both functions within a single conversation without switching interfaces or re-establishing context, reflecting the natural flow of patient healthcare administrative interactions."),
        H2("1.8 Technical Foundation"),
        S("The system is implemented entirely in Python, using the LangChain ecosystem for language model integration and tool management, and the LangGraph framework for state machine-based conversation flow orchestration. The large language model component is Meta's Llama 3.3 70B parameter model in its instruction-tuned variant, accessed through the Groq inference API which provides low-latency cloud-hosted inference suitable for interactive conversational applications. Groq's hardware-accelerated inference infrastructure, based on Language Processing Units designed for transformer model inference, delivers response latencies compatible with conversational interaction, typically under two seconds for most patient queries."),
        S("The customer support knowledge base uses FAISS, an open-source vector similarity search library developed by Meta AI Research, to index embedding vectors computed from hospital document passages using the all-MiniLM-L6-v2 sentence transformer model from HuggingFace. This embedding model produces 384-dimensional dense vector representations of text passages that capture semantic meaning, enabling the retrieval of relevant passages even when the patient's query does not use the exact words appearing in the document. Retrieval results are passed to the Llama 3.3 70B model with a synthesis prompt that instructs it to generate a factual answer grounded only in the provided passages and to acknowledge when information is not available in the knowledge base rather than generating unsupported responses."),
        H2("1.9 Scope and Delimitations"),
        S("This research is delimited in three ways. First, the system is designed and evaluated as a prototype in a controlled test environment rather than as a deployed production system with real patients. The evaluation evidence speaks to the system's technical capabilities in controlled testing scenarios, not to its real-world performance with a heterogeneous patient population under operational conditions. Second, the system supports English and Swahili as its two primary languages of interaction. Support for Kenyan regional languages such as Kikuyu, Dholuo, and Kamba is outside the scope of the current design, though the architecture's modular language handling is designed to facilitate future language extension. Third, the customer support function is limited to information contained in the hospital knowledge base, which is built from official KUTRRH documents available at the time of system development. The system cannot answer questions about events, changes, or policies that postdate the knowledge base documents, and it is designed to acknowledge this limitation explicitly rather than generating potentially incorrect information from the language model's general training knowledge."),
        H2("1.10 Conceptual Framework"),
        S("The conceptual framework integrates four theoretical anchors that collectively explain the design choices made in the artifact. Queuing theory provides the mathematical foundation for the queue congestion prediction model, offering classical analytical results about arrival rates, service rates, server utilization, and expected waiting time distributions that are adapted for the appointment slot recommendation function. Language technology theory provides the foundation for understanding the capabilities and limitations of large language models in multilingual conversational settings, motivating both the language detection approach and the deterministic localization template design. Information retrieval theory, specifically the vector space model and dense neural retrieval approaches, provides the foundation for the retrieval-augmented generation customer support component, including the embedding-based similarity search and the synthesis prompt design that grounds LLM responses in retrieved document content. Design science theory provides the epistemological foundation for treating the artifact and its evaluation as a form of scholarly knowledge production, framing the design decisions and their evaluation outcomes as contributions to the information systems design knowledge base."),
        H2("1.11 Dual-Function Architecture Rationale"),
        S("A central design choice of this research is the integration of appointment management and customer support within a single conversational interface rather than building two separate systems. This integration choice is motivated by the patient experience: from the patient's perspective, a single hospital assistant that can answer all their questions and perform all their administrative transactions is more useful, more trustworthy, and less cognitively demanding than a system that requires them to know in advance which type of question they are asking. The integration also creates natural efficiencies: a patient who begins by asking about a department's services can be guided seamlessly into appointment booking for that department without switching interfaces or repeating their context. A patient who encounters an unexpected fee schedule can ask the customer support function about payment options within the same conversation in which they are booking their appointment."),
        S("The integration requires a routing mechanism that correctly identifies whether an incoming query is transactional (requiring the deterministic booking workflow) or informational (requiring the retrieval-augmented generation customer support pathway). Misrouting in either direction produces a suboptimal outcome: routing an appointment booking query to the information retrieval pathway produces an unhelpful factual response where a booking workflow should have been initiated, and routing a hospital information query to the appointment booking workflow produces inappropriate prompts for patient demographic details where a factual answer should have been provided. The intent detection logic described in Chapter 4 is designed to route correctly in both directions with high reliability."),
        H2("1.12 Five-Dimension Analytical Framework"),
        S("The evaluation of the artifact is structured around five analytical dimensions that together provide a comprehensive assessment of the system's fitness for healthcare deployment. Accessibility evaluates the degree to which the system can be used by the intended patient population, including patients with varying language preferences, varying digital literacy levels, and varying familiarity with hospital terminology, for both appointment and customer support interactions. Reliability evaluates the consistency of correct transactional outcomes for appointment management functions, with specific attention to booking completion rates and guardrail effectiveness. Optimization evaluates the value added by the queue congestion prediction and slot recommendation functions. Trust evaluates the degree to which the system's outputs in both appointment management and customer support contexts are accurate, appropriately scoped, and consistent with patient expectations of a healthcare service. This dimension is particularly important for the customer support function, where factual accuracy of hospital information responses is essential for patient decision-making. Governance evaluates the degree to which the system's design satisfies the audit, data protection, scope, escalation, and language equity requirements identified in the governance analysis."),
        H2("1.13 Thesis Structure"),
        S("The thesis is organized into seven chapters. Chapter 1 presents the introduction, problem statement, research objectives, research questions, significance, and conceptual framework. Chapter 2 reviews the relevant literature across six domains: conversational AI and healthcare chatbots, retrieval-augmented generation and knowledge-base systems, multilingual natural language processing, queue management theory and applications, digital health equity in sub-Saharan Africa, and AI governance and responsible deployment. Chapter 3 describes the research methodology, covering the design science research approach, the case context, the artifact design cycles, the evaluation strategy, and the research quality considerations. Chapter 4 presents the system design and implementation, covering the five-layer architecture, the appointment management subsystem with deterministic guardrails, the retrieval-augmented generation customer support subsystem, the intent routing mechanism, the multilingual localization approach, the queue prediction model, and the governance controls. Chapter 5 reports the evaluation results across the five analytical dimensions for both appointment management and customer support functions. Chapter 6 discusses the results in relation to the research questions and the broader literature. Chapter 7 presents conclusions, implementation recommendations for healthcare institutions, policy recommendations, and directions for future research."),
    ]
    return title, sections
